{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"875747315592fe66a07224b224803de292d5bf92207a7a95418e6ca6582c4cc8","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_c23ce4af255b08f376928afed486542c8256a174ab146b6a2d574b5dcf6f3489","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a method for learning dense, incremental reward functions from a small number of visual demonstrations by leveraging pre-trained deep features. The primary originality lies in the combination of unsupervised temporal segmentation and discriminative feature selection to identify sub-goals, enabling cross-embodiment imitation (human to robot). The work is positioned as a novel approach to reward learning that avoids the need for explicit sub-goal supervision or robot-state demonstrations, offering a practical solution for real-world robotic manipulation tasks.","paper_id":"Bkul3t9ee","paper_identity_sha256":"8d41641dfc20292b23b375f32202acc20930f89a398323b59d87aca3cdc5f1f6","prompt_identity_sha256":"3c6f3746a8291f04f6cc0e538ce42ce8034731be6dda8cb3839fdabd287a3ace","raw_response_sha256":"e75140a4e1e9d06ddc407be0be19722c55953d05025c15bc9445b8893b248d82","request_identity_sha256":"fd264d93dfe26f8b20ca7d01ccb266d23a60a218245a404c212bacea66b237a0","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"11a8a2a472bd3f1bcb618fd551d9a76ef0030fbdfef61d15e8f9cddb11868c72","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002","SRC-003"],"review_text":"The paper frames the problem of reward function design in reinforcement learning as a significant barrier to real-world deployment, specifically highlighting the difficulty of measuring intermediate steps in multi-step tasks. It proposes a new problem formulation focused on automatically identifying key intermediate steps and discriminative features from a small number of demonstration sequences without explicit sub-goal supervision. This formulation is presented as a way to leverage the abstraction power of deep models to infer perceptual reward functions, addressing the limitation of sparse or manually engineered rewards.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-009","SRC-012","SRC-015","SRC-016"],"review_text":"The methodology introduces a two-stage algorithm: unsupervised discovery of steps via recursive similarity maximization (minimizing intra-segment variance) and feature selection using a scoring heuristic based on the distance between positive and negative frame statistics. The reward function is constructed as a log-probability under a naive Bayes style model over the top-n selected features. While the individual components (variance minimization, feature ranking, naive Bayes) are standard, the specific pipeline for deriving dense, incremental rewards from pre-trained deep features for robotic manipulation is presented as a novel algorithmic approach that does not require explicit sub-goal labels.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-010","SRC-014","SRC-019","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper provides an analysis of the generalizability of pre-trained deep visual features for representing goals and sub-goals in manipulation tasks. It demonstrates that sparse subsets of features from mid and high-level layers of a pre-trained Inception network are sufficient to discriminate between task steps without retraining. The qualitative analysis of reward functions for pouring and door opening tasks shows that the learned rewards are continuous, incremental, and robust to variations in appearance and embodiment (e.g., human vs. robot), providing insight into the utility of off-the-shelf deep features for reward learning.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-018","SRC-021","SRC-023","SRC-025","SRC-028"],"review_text":"The experimental setup includes a quantitative evaluation of unsupervised step discovery and reward function accuracy using Jaccard similarity against a random baseline, as well as a real-world robotic validation on a door opening task. The paper introduces a specific protocol for comparing visual rewards against a ground-truth IMU-based reward, demonstrating that the learned perceptual rewards can achieve comparable success rates. The use of human video demonstrations for training a robot's reward function is highlighted as a novel experimental condition that validates the cross-embodiment capability of the method.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-008","SRC-009"],"review_text":"The paper combines pre-trained deep visual features with unsupervised temporal segmentation and discriminative feature selection to create a reward learning method. The rationale for this combination is that pre-trained features provide general prior knowledge about the visual world, while the segmentation and selection steps allow for the identification of task-specific sub-goals without explicit supervision. This combination is presented as a way to overcome the limitations of previous methods that required kinesthetic demonstrations or low-dimensional state spaces, enabling efficient vision-based reward learning from human videos.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":[],"review_text":"The paper does not present negative results or critical assessments of existing methods as a primary contribution. While it discusses the shortcomings of target-image based rewards and the challenges of scaling IRL to high-dimensional systems, these are used to motivate the proposed method rather than to provide a systematic failure analysis or negative result. The evaluation focuses on the positive performance of the proposed method compared to baselines.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"b4157f901c845edc50f52f953a331a896b522329ff45ecf789d1947fa8e13768","paper_id":"Bkul3t9ee","paper_ordinal":63,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"e75140a4e1e9d06ddc407be0be19722c55953d05025c15bc9445b8893b248d82","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"Bkul3t9ee","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"f29ec438d83c1b354bf422c3a008d52de6ae59750144c33d56c77e733436a1de","request_identity_sha256":"fd264d93dfe26f8b20ca7d01ccb266d23a60a218245a404c212bacea66b237a0","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1154,"prompt_tokens":13698,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":14852},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"fd264d93dfe26f8b20ca7d01ccb266d23a60a218245a404c212bacea66b237a0","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}